From 3bee74d85e3fe61e82d74aef1fe0e1dc36c9d4de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 07:36:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08109/11362] The shipped engine re-ask gate never fired once
 in 169 induce calls

The worry was that the shipped engine-defect re-ask gate carries the same
defect the goal variant did: `attempt < tries - 1` stops only the LAST
attempt from continuing out of the loop, so an EARLIER re-ask can spend the
attempt that would have been the accept. On the goal variant that converted
accepts into hard failures on 17 of 21 cells.

Measured on the live induce path, 20 games x 2 replicates x 4 arms = 160
cells, one server, paired seeds, primary = usable MINUS hard failures
(clustered at the GAME level; usable alone is the metric that hid this):

  arm                    usable  defective  hard-fail  net
  a_off      gate OFF        38          2          0   38
  b_shipped  SHIPPED         39          0          1   38
  c_owns     +fix            39          0          1   38
  aa         A/A floor       37          1          2   35

net is IDENTICAL across the three arms. The A/A floor moves it by 3, i.e.
more than any treatment contrast. All 160 cells measurable, none missing.

THE REASON IS EXPOSURE, AND IT IS THE FINDING: the gate fired ZERO times.
Only 3 of 160 emitted engines were mechanically defective (1.9%) against the
61% (22/36) its 13/36 -> 22/36 headline was measured at. A supplementary
counterfactual restoring the pre-2026-07-31 payload (repeat_penalty=1.0)
made induction far more fragile -- 4 hard failures in 9 cells against 1 in
160 -- and the gate STILL did not fire, because the dominant failure there
is a CONTENT failure (no parseable code) and the gate runs only after the
code parses. Combined: 0 firings in 169 induce calls.

So the gate is neither net-positive nor net-negative. It is INERT, and its
headline no longer describes the shipped stack. The latent defect in
generate() is NOT cleared -- exposure depends on model, prompt and repeat
penalty, all of which change -- so it is dormant, not absent.

Two things this run does NOT establish, stated because the raw numbers
invite both errors:
  * arm C is UNTESTED, not validated. The fix only alters the loop when a
    re-ask fires; none did, so C executed B's path (30 of 39 engines
    byte-identical).
  * the counterfactual's arm gap (net +3 gate-off vs -1 gate-on) is NOT a
    treatment effect. engine_defect_reasks_delta is 0 in every arm there, so
    the gate never acted; it is request-position sampling variance, and that
    condition has no A/A floor to bound it.

Internal validity: at identical prompt and seed, b_shipped vs c_owns came
back 77% byte-identical while a_off vs b/c came back 21%. The seed works;
request position 1 pays a cold prompt cache. That degrades the nominal
pairing to independent draws without biasing an arm, which is exactly why
the A/A floor is the operative yardstick here.

Min reachable p was pre-registered before the first LLM call: 1.9e-6 at 20
games, and p<=0.05 unreachable below 6 discordant games. Observed 3
discordant (min reachable 0.25), so the primary is reported as a null that
the design could not have called significant either way.

No shipped default changed. adversarial_verify 0-flagged. results/arc_e3
verified unchanged before and after. No scored or online ARC game played.

